01 |\n39\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Filter and sort deployed models list by status | RHAISTRAT-9001-E001 |\n40\t| HLR-5 | P2 | Product documentation for status values and Failed troubleshooting | RHAISTRAT-9001-E002 |\n41\t\n42\tAll five HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n43\t\n44\t## Decomposition Notes\n45\t\n46\t- **Triage (Step 0):** Not below-threshold. Although the implementation scope is a single active component owned by a single team with strong AI-implementability signals, HLR-5 requires substantive documentation authoring (a new product documentation topic explaining status values and troubleshooting steps) — a distinct work stream from code implementation that warrants its own PR review cycle. Check 2 (docs-only) does not fire because the Dashboard requires code changes.\n47\t- **KServe is a passive component.** The Affected Components list includes KServe, but the strategy states KServe already exposes the needed InferenceService status conditions and events; no KServe code changes are needed (confirmed by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, which documents `Ready`/`PredictorReady` status conditions and Kubernetes events as existing interfaces). Validation that the Dashboard's status mapping matches the actual InferenceService status conditions and events is therefore an acceptance criterion on the nearest active component's epic (RHAISTRAT-9001-E001). The strategy assigns no validation role to the KServe owning team, so no separate validation epic is generated.\n48\t- **Priority split not applied to the Dashboard epic.** HLR-3 (P1, 30-second refresh) and HLR-4 (P2, filter/sort) share the same UI surface and data (the model serving views and the deployed-models status data) as the P0 work in HLR-1/HLR-2. Per Step 4 they are tightly coupled to the same implementation surface and stay bundled in one epic at P0 rather than being split by priority label.\n49\t- **Component mapping:** epic components are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`: `Dashboard` for E001, `Documentation` for E002. The strategy's \"KServe\" affected component maps to no epic because it is passive (see above).\n50\t- **No Investigation epics.** The strategy's Open Questions section states \"None blocking\"; no conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or risks tables are present. No unknown changes which downstream epics exist or what they do.\n51\t- **Team assumption for E002:** the strategy contains no Impacted Teams table. The documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI Dashboard, the team that owns the feature being documented and performs the mandatory technical review; adjust if a dedicated documentation team owns this content.\n52\t\n53\t## Health Warnings\n54\t\n55\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy omits the Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then with \"measured by\" clauses), and Non-Functional Requirements sections. The decomposition proceeded from the HLR table, Affected Components list, Out of Scope list, and Open Questions; per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n56\t- **Out-of-scope boundary relied on for ACs:** alerting/notifications, status history/timeline views, and ModelMesh deployments are explicitly out of scope; acceptance criteria on E001 enforce that the status surface covers KServe InferenceService deployments only.\n57\t- No priority inversions detected. No scope traps detected (no infrastructure implied beyond existing Dashboard and KServe components, both present in architecture context).\n58\t\n59\t## Tiered Delivery\n60\t\n61\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing. Both affected components are in the platform (architecture context files exist for Dashboard and KServe), and no upstream contributions are required.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-870f01c376fda592","agent_id":"ab4d9bd43bd0e0d00","session_id":"43f64084-1d69-4055-a8f7-bc56aec5e245","uuid":"5fe769e0-e