\t\n45\tAll five HLRs map to epics and the traceability matrix is present and accurate:\n46\t\n47\t- HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4 → RHAISTRAT-9001-E001\n48\t- HLR-5 → RHAISTRAT-9001-E002\n49\t\n50\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001 blocks no P0 work; E002 is a\n51\t`docs-authoring` epic, which is exempt from priority inheritance and correctly\n52\tderives P1 from the strategy's `priority: Major`.\n53\t\n54\tHowever, E001 collapses three priority tiers into one epic. HLR-3 (P1,\n55\t30-second auto-refresh without manual reload) and HLR-4 (P2, filter/sort by\n56\tstatus) are distinct deferrable features, not incidental polish on the P0\n57\tstatus surface:\n58\t\n59\t- HLR-4 (filter/sort controls on the deployed models list) can ship\n60\t  independently of — and long after — the P0 status column and detail view; the\n61\t  strategy's P2 label signals exactly that deferrability.\n62\t- HLR-3 (auto-refresh within 30 seconds) is a separable polling/refresh\n63\t  mechanism, not a byproduct of displaying status; the strategy's P1 label\n64\t  marks it deferrable relative to the P0 surface.\n65\t\n66\tBundling them at P0 means the P0 status surface cannot ship without the P2\n67\tfilter/sort work being complete. The decomposition notes justify the bundle as\n68\t\"tightly coupled to the same implementation surface,\" but shared-surface\n69\tconvenience does not override independent deferrability, which is a planning\n70\trequirement. The correct shape is separate epics (at minimum HLR-4 split out;\n71\tHLR-3 should also be independently plannable), each traceable to its own HLRs.\n72\tThis is a major issue and forces this criterion to 0.\n73\t\n74\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n75\t\n76\t- Single edge E001 → E002, matching E002's frontmatter `dependencies:\n77\t  [RHAISTRAT-9001-E001]` and the summary DAG diagram (arrow drawn dependency →\n78\t  dependent, per convention). E001 correctly has no dependencies.\n79\t- The edge is a valid Rule 11 docs-last edge: the docs-authoring epic is\n80\t  blocked by the (single) Implementation epic, confirmed by the contract\n81\t  validator. Per the docs-authoring exception it does not propagate priority,\n82\t  which the summary states correctly.\n83\t- No circular dependencies, no invalid edges, no konflux-onboarding /\n84\t  license-validation epics (so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints do not\n85\t  apply), no conditional branch files, no image-digest/endpoint configuration\n86\t  references misfiled as DAG edges.\n87\t- Critical path length 2 matches the S-size heuristic (1-2) for a\n88\t  single-active-component strategy.\n89\t- No data/artifact dependency is missing from the DAG: E002 consumes only\n90\t  E001's implemented surface, which is captured by the existing edge.\n91\t\n92\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n93\t\n94\t- Component/team tuples are correct and separate: E001 (Dashboard / RHOAI\n95\t  Dashboard) and E002 (Documentation / RHOAI Dashboard). Both component names\n96\t  are in the canonical list (validator-confirmed), and the Dashboard team\n97\t  ownership matches `dashboard.md` architecture context.\n98\t- KServe is correctly treated as passive — the strategy states the needed\n99\t  conditions/events already exist, and `kserve.md` confirms Ready /\n100\t  PredictorReady conditions and Kubernetes events as existing interfaces. The\n101\t  status-mapping validation is correctly placed as an AC on E001 rather than a\n102\t  KServe epic.\n103\t- No scope duplication between epics: E001 is code implementation, E002 is\n104\t  content authoring — different work-product types correctly separated into\n105\t  different epics despite the same team.\n106\t- Minor observation (does not reduce the score): E001 bundles four HLRs' worth\n107\t  of scope (status derivation, list display, detail view, auto-refresh,\n108\t  filter/sort) and sits at the upper end of the ~2-week guideline; the priority\n109\t  split required by Criterion 1 would also bring it comfortably within size.\n110\t\n111\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n112\t\n113\t- Both epics are Implementation and both produce concrete artifacts (E001: the\n114\t  Dashboard status surface; E002: published documentation topics). No\n115\t  misclassification.\n116\t- No Investigation epics — correctly so: the strategy's Open Questions section\n117\t  states \"None blocking,\" and no unknown changes which downstream epics exist.\n118\t- `gated_by` / `gate_failure_impact` are null on both epics, consistent with\n119\t  the absence of gates.\n120\t\n121\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n122\t\n123\t- Type-appropriate signal fields: both epics are Implementation and carry\n124\t  `ai_signals` (not `investigation_signals`). Both ai-signals rationale files\n125\t  exist.\n126\t- E001 values are consistent with the strategy and the rubric: high\n127\t  change_specificity (exact statuses, views, refresh bound, data source),\n128\t  pattern_precedent justified by the Dashboard's existing status views\n129\t  (confirmed in `dashboard.md`), existing_foundation +1 (extends existing model\n130\t  serving views), open_questions +1 (strategy states none blocking),\n131\t  architecture_claims +1 (substantiated by `kserve.md`), repo_access +1\n132\t  (odh-dashboard upstream is public).\n133\t- E002 values are consistent: pattern_precedent 0 (no prior\n134\t  status-values/troubleshooting topic cited), human_process_gates -1 (Red Hat\n135\t  docs editorial/technical review process) is the right call for docs-authoring\n136\t  work.\n137\t- Rationales in both ai-signals files match the frontmatter values. (Per\n138\t  instructions, the computed `ai_implementability` fields were not checked.)\n139\t\n140\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n141\t\n142\t- E001 has testable Given/When/Then ACs derived from HLR-1 through HLR-4, plus\n143\t  explicit out-of-scope enforcement ACs (no alerts/history/timeline; KServe\n144\t  InferenceService only) that mirror the strategy's Out of Scope list.\n145\t- E0